<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 10 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">West Africa's Golden Age</h1><p class="path-tagline">The empires that grew rich on the Saharan gold and salt trade, and their southern neighbours.</p><p class="path-intro">Caravan routes across the Sahara carried gold, slaves and ivory north and salt, horses and textiles south, and with them came Islam and literacy. Ghana, Mali and Songhai grew rich on that trade, and under Mali and Songhai Timbuktu became a centre of Islamic learning whose scholars were famous across the Islamic world. Further east, Kanem-Bornu tied the Lake Chad basin to the route to Tripoli, and the walled Hausa city-kingdoms linked the Sahara with the forest. To the south, Benin's royal guilds cast the Benin Bronzes, Oyo's cavalry made it the leading power between the Niger and the Volta, and the Asante were bound together by the Golden Stool.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Navigator</a> · <a href="/start">the Builder</a> · <a href="/start">the Strategist</a></p><p class="path-print-row"><button type="button" class="ws-print path-print">Print this path</button></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/trans-saharan-trade"><span class="path-step-ph">1</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Encyclopedia · Term</span><span class="path-step-name">Trans-Saharan trade</span><span class="path-step-why">Caravans carried gold, slaves and ivory north across the Sahara and salt, horses and textiles south, and this trade underpinned the wealth of Ghana, Mali and Songhai.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The trans-Saharan trade was the network of caravan routes across the Sahara Desert linking North Africa and the Mediterranean with West and Central Africa.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/koumbi-saleh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/koumbi-saleh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Encyclopedia · Place</span><span class="path-step-name">Koumbi Saleh</span><span class="path-step-why">Widely identified as the capital of the Ghana Empire, a twin city of a Muslim merchant quarter and a royal town, and a major centre of the gold and salt trade.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Koumbi Saleh is an archaeological site in south-eastern Mauritania widely identified as the capital of the Ghana Empire.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/mali-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/djenne.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Profile · 1235 CE – 1600 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Mali Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Founded by Sundiata Keita, Mali sent the gold of Bambuk and Bure into the Saharan trade, and in 1324 Mansa Musa's pilgrimage to Mecca dazzled Cairo with it.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Mali Empire was a West African empire founded by Sundiata Keita around 1235, which flourished until the fifteenth century.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/timbuktu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Encyclopedia · Place</span><span class="path-step-name">Timbuktu</span><span class="path-step-why">A city near the Niger at the southern edge of the Sahara, a centre of trade and Islamic learning under Mali that reached its height under Songhai.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Timbuktu is a city near the Niger River in present-day Mali, at the southern edge of the Sahara.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/songhai-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gao.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Profile · 1430 CE – 1600 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Songhai Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">The largest empire in West African history held Gao, Timbuktu and Djenné on the Niger Bend, until a Moroccan army with firearms routed it at Tondibi in 1591.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Songhai Empire was a West African state centred on Gao on the middle Niger, which expanded in the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries under Sunni Ali and Askia Muhammad.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/kanem-bornu-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sayf-ibn-dhi-yazan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Profile · 700 CE – 1893 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Kanem-Bornu Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Ruled for close to eight centuries by the Sayfawa dynasty, this Lake Chad empire held the route to Tripoli and tied the central Sudan to the Muslim world.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Kanem–Bornu Empire was a long-lived state around Lake Chad, first centred in Kanem and later in Bornu.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/hausa-kingdoms"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gobarau-minaret.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Profile · 1000 CE – 1808 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Hausa kingdoms</span><span class="path-step-why">Walled city-kingdoms such as Kano, Katsina and Zazzau never united, but shared a language, court institutions and a trade network linking the Sahara and the forest.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/benin-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Profile · 1180 CE – 1897 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Benin Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">From Benin City the Edo Obas built a regional empire from the 15th century, and royal guilds cast the Benin Bronzes, looted by a British expedition in 1897.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Benin Empire, also called the Kingdom of Benin, was a state in the forest region of present-day southern Nigeria ruled by the oba from Benin City.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/oyo-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yorubaland.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Profile · 1300 CE – 1896 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Oyo Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Its cavalry made this Yoruba savannah state the leading power between the Niger and the Volta, and in the 18th century it forced Dahomey into vassalage.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Oyo Empire was a Yoruba state in the savannah of present-day western Nigeria, founded according to tradition by Oranmiyan.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/golden-stool"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/golden-stool.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">10 · Encyclopedia · Work</span><span class="path-step-name">Golden Stool</span><span class="path-step-why">The sacred throne of the Asante, believed to hold the soul of the nation; the British governor's demand of 1900 that it be surrendered provoked a war.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Golden Stool is the sacred royal throne and symbol of the Ashanti Empire, believed to contain the soul of the Asante nation.</span></span></a></li></ol>
